WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.189 --> 00:00:07.939
The oldest person to have ever lived was a
French woman, Jeanne Calment, who lived to

00:00:07.939 --> 00:00:12.379
the age of 122 years and 164 days.

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:18.070
As modern medicine and living standards improve
so does our life expectancy, but will the

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.940
day soon come when life has no cap?

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:32.640
Some scientists believe it to be much more
than science fiction.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.120
To understand how humans can live forever,
we first need to understand what ageing actually

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.120
is.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.969
A lot of people think ageing is a very complex
process, but it’s really quite simple.

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:49.640
Ageing is basically a side effect of being
alive, all of our bodies’ normal day-to-day

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:55.489
processes such as breathing, moving and digesting
food, gradually cause wear and tear on our

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:56.489
cells.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:02.510
And after a certain amount of wear and tear,
our cells die, causing permanent damage to

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.530
our bodies.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:09.150
Our bodies can only take so much of this cell
damage, so eventually, we die.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:13.780
Think of your body like a car, driving it
around causes unavoidable wear and tear to

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:19.100
all of its components, until one day it gives
up and refuses to start.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.020
Unless you perform regular maintenance on
it such as changing the oil or even replacing

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.020
entire parts.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.510
Some revolutionary scientists have taken this
same mechanical view on the human body and

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:36.250
believe that with regular maintenance and
even replacing damaged organs, we can keep

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.360
our bodies running smoothly indefinitely.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:47.100
In 2011 surgeons in Sweden performed the world’s
first organ transplant using a synthetic organ

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.920
that was grown in a lab.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.590
The organ was grown by London scientists in
just a few days.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:59.240
But most importantly the synthetic windpipe
was grown using the patients own DNA, meaning

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.350
there was no chance of the body rejecting
the new organ.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.140
The 36-year-old cancer patient is now doing
fine after the transplant.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.690
Scientists are now working on doing the same
thing with complex organs such as hearts and

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:18.610
lungs and they predict that in less than 10
years any organ will be able to be grown in

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:24.470
a lab on demand in unlimited quantities without
the need for donors.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.490
But immortality could potentially manifest
itself in many ways.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:36.959
Biogerontologist Marios Kyriazis believes
that immortality is not an if but a when,

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:42.740
he theorises that immortality is an inevitable
consequence of evolution.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:48.080
His theory states that through evolution the
human brain will one day become so complex

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.350
it will be capable of sustaining our bodies
for an indefinite amount of time.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:58.790
By slowing down the ageing process and stopping
it completely when the body is fully-grown.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:05.450
But evolution is a an awfully slow process,
if you’re looking for a more immediate solution

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.180
to eternal youth you may be surprised to hear
there’s a lot more research going on in

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.830
this field than you might imagine.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:20.099
In 2005 a british scientist named Aubrey de
Grey setup a research foundation called SENS,

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:25.260
which stands for “Strategies for Engineered
Negligible Senescence”.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:31.610
The aim of SENS is to research and develop
a diverse range of regenerative medical therapies,

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:38.230
to not only stop the ageing process in humans
but also reverse it.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:44.900
Aubrey believes that death is not an inevitability
but merely a disease, which can be cured with

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.069
the correct medical treatment.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:51.750
In order to lengthen our lives we simply have
to extend the length of our telomeres, which

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:58.270
are caps at the ends of our DNA that shorten
as we age leading to the breakdown of cells.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:04.220
The answer is simple, if we can develop a
pill to lengthen our telomeres we can stop

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:07.270
and even reverse the ageing process.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:13.300
Aubrey believes that we will have realistic
age-preventative medicine available to us

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:19.930
in as little as 25 years and that the first
person who will live to 1,000 yeas old has

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:21.720
already been born.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:28.270
But Aubrey de Grey isn’t the only scientist
interested in living forever.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:34.260
Scientist and director of engineering at Google
Ray Kurzweil is a prominent futurist and has

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.720
accurately predicted many of today’s advanced
technologies years in advance.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.820
Because our understanding of genes and computer
technology is accelerating at an incredible

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.470
rate, Ray made a bold prediction.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:54.139
That in just 20 years time humans will have
developed super advanced microscopic nano

00:04:54.139 --> 00:05:00.280
bots that will be able to enter our bodies
in their millions and perform the same jobs

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:08.160
as our blood cells but a thousand times more
effectively, keeping our bodies 100% infection

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.590
free and instantly repairing any damaged cells.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:16.889
Enabling us to live indefinitely and even
reverse ageing.

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:21.840
But Ray predicts that this is not all that
nanotechnology will be able to achieve in

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:28.540
the very near future, he predicts within 25
years we will be able to use nanotechnology

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.550
to modify our bodies and obtain superhuman
powers.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:37.800
For example we will be able to go scuba-diving
for hours without oxygen.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.150
We will be able to extend our mental capacities
to such an extent we will be able to write

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.840
books within minutes.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:51.240
And in our daily lives, hologram like figures
will pop into our brain to explain what’s

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.240
happening.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.449
The potentials for nanotechnology are limitless
and many scientists agree that at our current

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:05.139
rate of technological advancement it won’t
be long before nanotechnology and nano bots

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:08.030
are part of our everyday lives.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:12.730
Changing the way and for how long we live
our lives.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:18.930
But if all else fails you can always freeze
your body after you die and hope that sometime

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:24.280
in the future scientists will be able to revive
your frozen cadaver and bring you back to

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.520
life.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:31.290
This is called cryonics, you may have only
seen it in films but it’s actually a real

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.290
science.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:37.030
The process involves refrigerating the body
in liquid nitrogen and replacing the blood

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:42.509
with a cryo-protectant fluid to prevent ice
crystals forming inside the body and damaging

00:06:42.509 --> 00:06:43.660
the cells.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:49.889
The body is then suspended upside down in
giant steel container so the head stays the

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:53.780
coldest, and the temperature is lowered to
minus

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.520
196 centigrade.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:03.910
There are currently over 250 bodies preserved
in this way, waiting for science to revive

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.780
them and more than 1,000 living people have
signed up for the procedure.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:14.940
But it doesn’t come cheap, it costs around
$200,000 for a full-body preservation.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.759
Although you can get just your head preserved
for slightly cheaper, in the hope that one

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:24.919
day science will be advanced enough to connect
your head to another body and bring it back

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:28.500
to life with all your memories preserved.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:33.419
All this talk of immortality is very exciting,
and may be a lot closer than you think.

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:38.490
But what will happen when a certain portion
of humanity refuses to die?

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.460
It’s commonly a topic of heated debate.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.419
Overpopulation is already a global issue,
until we are able to find other planets to

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:51.580
inhabit, the unfortunate truth remains that
we need people to die.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.860
Earth can only support so many people.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.290
But that’s not the only problem.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:02.620
1% of the world’s population own 50% of
the world’s wealth, and if those few people

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:08.500
refuse to die there won’t be any more wealth
distribution, they will only continue to further

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:15.060
monopolise the global market and the rich
will become richer whilst the poor grow poorer.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:20.100
Another caveat is the retirement age would
shoot up to hundreds of years.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:25.861
This is fine for the ambitious few who seek
premium jobs and luxurious lifestyles, but

00:08:25.861 --> 00:08:32.340
imagine being forced to work for over 1,000
years flipping burgers at McDonalds.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:38.370
In an immortal society criminal punishments
would become greatly devalued. 30 years behind

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:43.729
bars in nothing for someone with a lifespan
of thousands of years, and with such a lousy

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:47.420
deterrent crime could increase dramatically.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:52.089
Also with the value of ones life being so
much higher death sentences wouldn’t be

00:08:52.089 --> 00:08:57.930
given out so frequently and we may have to
invest heavily in rehabilitation techniques

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:04.090
or even brainwashing techniques to keep the
unruly side of society under control.

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:08.010
But there are benefits to an immortal population.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:13.430
We will finally be able to send people to
explore other planets, that would otherwise

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.460
take more than a lifetime to reach.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:22.380
Also those Einstein-like people among us who
have brilliant minds will live forever and

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.800
so their knowledge will continue to grow and
contribute to the global knowledge bank.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.279
As a result, human knowledge will grow at
an unprecedented rate.

00:09:32.279 --> 00:09:48.360
Society will advance and develop new technologies

00:09:48.360 --> 00:10:02.069
at an astonishing, ever-increasing rate.

